issue.body:
If you have a OneTimeSetup method in a base class that calls Assert.Inconclusive() to stop the test then in 4.3.2 the OneTimeSetup in the derived class would not be called. With 4.4.0 the OneTimeSetup in the derived class is now being called. This seems to be in contrast to the documentation - https://docs.nunit.org/articles/nunit/writing-tests/setup-teardown/index.html

```
public class BaseClass
{
  [OneTimeSetup]
  public void BaseSetup()
  {
    Assert.Inconclusive("Skipping");
  }
}

public class ChildClass : BaseClass
{
  [OneTimeSetup]
  public void ChildSetup()
  {
    Assert.Fail("This should never be called");
  }
}

```

The following code behaves differently when executed with nunit 4.3.2 and 4.4.0:

```
using NUnit.Framework;

namespace UnitTests;

[TestFixture]
public class MyTestClass
{
    [SetUp]
    public void SetUp()
    {
        Assert.Inconclusive("Test state is inconclusive!");
    }

    [Test]
    public void MyTest()
    {
        Assert.Fail("This should not be executed!");
    }
}
```

- With nunit 4.3.2 MyTest() will not be executed and the result is "Inconclusive".
- With nunit 4.4.0 MyTest() will be executed and the result is "Failed".

This change results in failures in my tests.
I saw https://github.com/nunit/nunit/pull/5029 that seems to have something to do with the change.
I am not sure if the change is expected or not.
